\begin{itemize}
	\item exchangeable NH4, total Kjeldahl-N, dissolved NO3 and NO2, exchangeable orthophosphate, total-P, millipore filtered vs. unfiltered samples, point centered quarter sampling technique \cite{mitchell_quantitative_2010}, throughfall, 
	\item nutrient dynamics in ag (corn field) watershed upstream of broad leaved deciduous trees, Maryland; compare pathways of nutrient flux within and between habitats
	\item examine N,C,P transformations; samples from surface and shallow subsurface waters
	\item 1680 kg/ha limestone (April); 2-10-13 fertilizer (April); pre-emergent herbicide (May); liquid N (May); urea (July)
	\item 105 kg/ha N (63\% organic, 22\% NH4+, 15\% NO3-) PLUS 14 kg/ha N (46\% organic, 20\% NH4+, 34\% NO3-)
	\item spring input 13.9 kg/ha C ( bulk precip) BUT spring watershed discharge 153 kg/ha C (death of soil biota) ==> every other season has C input proportional to rain but substantially less C discharge (eg, fall C bulk precip 5.7 kg/ha; watershed discharge C 0.42 kg/ha)
	\item pulse of C in Spring because killed soil biota !!
	\item Corn: nutrient uptake $\approx  250$ kg N/ha/yr and $\approx 45$ kg P/ha/yr; NPP $\approx  22,000$ kg C/ha/yr
	\item 28\% of N  and 23\% P exported by corn harvest
	\item N retention poor in disturbed ecosystems ($\approx 10\%$); N retention excellent in undisturbed forest ecosystems ($\approx 90\%$)
	\item P retention average in disturbed ecosystems ($\approx 40\%$); P retention very good in undisturbed forest ecosystems ($\approx 80\%$)
	\item groundwater (subsurface flow)  the major pathway of N loss from both crop filed and forest
	\item forest throughfall N ==> $\approx 70\%$ retention by canopy
	\item forest throughfall P ==> $\approx 80\%$ supplemented by canopy
	\item Big question: What caused the decrease in groundwater NO3 concentration in the riparian forest? Samples pulled below rhizosphere (at 1 m). Peterjohn and Correl hypothesize only vegetative uptake and denitrification. But incremental growth accounts for only 33\%! Authors therefore default to denitrification. But bacteria now known to uptake nitrate and nitrite (nitrate and nitrite reductase enzymes)
	\item Riparian forest removed 4.1 Mg/ha particulates, 11 kg/ha particulate organic-N, 0.83 kg/ha NH4, 2.7 kg/ha NO3, 3 kg/ha particulate P  from surface runoff
	\item Riparian forest removed 45 kg/ha NO3 from subsurface flow
\end{itemize}
